# remote-cluster-agent: Project Rule

This project uses the `remote-cluster-agent` skill for development on a remote GPU cluster. This file is referenced from the project guide (`CLAUDE.md`, and `AGENTS.md` for Codex, usually as a symlink to `CLAUDE.md`) — these behavioral rules apply to **all** sessions here.

**Project key**: `{{PROJECT_KEY}}`

All concrete values (which node, remote dir, conda env, conda init script, mutagen session name, ignores) live in `~/.config/rca/config.toml` under `[projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}]` and `[nodes.<default_node>]` — **not in this file**. Look them up at the start of each session, then apply the rules below using those values.

Codex compatibility note: if the session started from Codex and you only saw `@.claude/rules/remote-cluster-agent.md` in `AGENTS.md`, explicitly read this file before running remote code. Do not assume `@...` references were expanded into context.

```bash
# Quick lookup (run once per session, cache the values):
rca config show
# Or just: Read ~/.config/rca/config.toml
```

Resolution order for any value:
1. Project-level (`[projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}].<field>`)
2. Falls back to node-level (`[nodes.<default_node>].<field>`) — for `dir`, `conda_env`, `conda_sh`
3. Falls back to global (`default_dir`, `agent_path`)

If a needed field is empty in all three, ask the user before guessing.

---

## Rule 1. Flush local→remote before remote exec

Mutagen syncs in near-real-time but is **not synchronous**. Local edits take a short window (sub-second to a few seconds) to land on the cluster. If you `rca exec` immediately after `Edit`, the cluster may run **stale code**.

**Fix**: flush before any `rca exec` that depends on a file you just edited.

```bash
mutagen sync flush <projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}.mutagen_session>
rca exec -n <projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}.default_node> "..."
```

`mutagen sync flush` blocks until propagation finishes. Cost: ~50-100ms when nothing pending, ~200-500ms after a small edit.

**Exempt** (no flush needed) — queries that don't touch files you just changed:
`nvidia-smi`, `ps`, `df`, `uptime`, `tmux ls`, `conda env list`, `rca nodes`.

> No "remote → local" flush race exists under one-way-replica — see Rule 4. To pull remote-generated files locally, use `rca cp`, not flush.

## Rule 2. Local edit / remote execute — never run user code locally

| Operation | Where |
|-----------|-------|
| `Read` / `Edit` / `Write` / `Glob` / `Grep` over **synced files** (code, configs, anything local owns) | **Local** (mutagen mirrors to remote) |
| `python ...`, `pytest`, `pip install`, training, scripts | **Remote** (`rca exec -n <node>`) |
| Read **remote-generated outputs** (training logs, checkpoints in ignored paths) | `rca cp <node>:<path> <local>` then `Read`, **or** `rca exec -n <node> "cat <path>"` for a quick peek (see Rule 4) |
| `nvidia-smi`, `ps`, status checks | **Remote** (`rca exec` or `rca batch`) |

The local machine does **not** have this project's runtime deps. Never run `python` / `pytest` / `pip` locally in this project.

## Rule 3. Python on remote must activate the project's conda env

Look up `conda_env` and `conda_sh` from config first, then use one of these forms:

```bash
# For long jobs / tests with live output (use -s to stream):
rca exec -s -n <node> --stdin <<'EOF'
source <conda_sh>
conda activate <conda_env>
cd <remote_dir>
python <your-command>
EOF

# One-liner for short commands (no streaming):
rca exec -n <node> -d <remote_dir> "conda run -n <conda_env> python <your-command>"
```

Without activation: `python` on remote uses the system interpreter and **will not find this project's deps** (ModuleNotFoundError).

## Rule 4. Sync direction is one-way-replica — mind the asymmetry

Mutagen mirrors local → remote only. Never the other way. Three implications the agent must internalize:

1. **Remote outputs do not appear locally automatically.** `Read("outputs/...")` only works for files local has written first. To read a file the cluster generated (logs, checkpoints, training results), use:
   ```bash
   rca cp <projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}.default_node>:<remote-path> <local-path>
   ```
   …then `Read` the local copy. For a quick peek without persisting, `rca exec -n <node> "cat <path>"`.

2. **Files written on remote in non-ignored paths get deleted by mutagen.** If a script writes `outputs/result.json` on the cluster and `outputs/` is **not** listed in `[projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}].ignores`, mutagen will treat that file as "stale" on beta and remove it on the next sync — silently. To preserve remote-generated outputs, the path / glob must be in the project's `ignores`. Check the live ignore list with `mutagen sync list <projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}.mutagen_session> --long`.

3. **No "remote → local" flush race exists.** `mutagen sync flush` only matters before `rca exec` (see Rule 1). After `rca exec`, there's nothing to flush — remote changes never come home on their own.

If a new path needs to survive on remote, add it to `[projects.{{PROJECT_KEY}}].ignores` in `~/.config/rca/config.toml` and rebuild the session via `bash <skill_dir>/mutagen-setup.sh ... <new-ignore>` (see skill's "Mutagen session health" for the full procedure).

---

To change the values, edit `~/.config/rca/config.toml` (or `rca config edit`). To regenerate this rule file, delete it and trigger the skill again — it will re-prompt and rewrite both this file and the corresponding config.toml entries.
